Exactly! 👍 Your understanding is basically correct. Just make one small correction.

### `append()`

`append()` can accept **Nodes/elements AND strings**, and it can accept **multiple values**.

```js
parent.append(node);
parent.append("Hello");
parent.append(node, "Hello", anotherNode);
```

For example:

```js id="q5h3p1"
let h1 = document.createElement("h1");
h1.innerText = "Hello";

document.body.append(h1, "Welcome", "Ketan");
```

All three are added.

---

### `appendChild()`

`appendChild()` accepts **only one Node** at a time.

```js id="6q7f3n"
document.body.appendChild(h1);
```

You cannot do:

```js id="zyb6i1"
document.body.appendChild(h1, p);
```

Only the first argument is used.

And you **cannot directly pass a string**:

```js id="j6f9u8"
document.body.appendChild("Hello");
```

❌ Error, because `"Hello"` is a string, not a Node.

---

### 🧠 Remember it like this

```text id="0olh14"
append()
   ↓
Node ✅
String ✅
Multiple values ✅

appendChild()
   ↓
Node ✅
String ❌
Only one Node at a time ✅
```

So your statement can be improved to:

> **`append()` can add multiple Nodes/elements and strings, while `appendChild()` accepts only one Node at a time.**

That's exactly the distinction you should remember.
